## 2.1.0 - 2026-10-30

### Added

- `python_accounting.utils.reporting_period_repair`, which finds and repairs the Reporting Periods and Balances that earlier versions left wrong for Entities whose year does not start in January (#38).

### Fixes

- For an Entity whose year does not start in January, the current Reporting Period follows `year_start`, and reports use the period containing their end date. Before the year start month these previously opened the next year's period early and rejected ordinary Transactions (#35, #36).
- The opening balance of a report or statement no longer counts Transactions dated on its start date twice, and a statement's opening balance now includes movement between the period start and its start date.
- A statement with no Transactions reports its opening balance as its closing balance, rather than 0.
- A Balance stays in its Reporting Period when it is edited, deleted or restored. Each of those previously moved it to the current period, taking it out of the opening balances of its own year. Balances already moved stay where they are, as their original period is not recorded. The date of an edited Balance is now checked against its own period rather than the current one.
- A report whose start date is before the Entity's first Reporting Period no longer raises `MissingReportingPeriodError`.
- `ReportingPeriod.date_year` without an Entity returns the year of the given date, rather than the current year.
- `python_accounting.reports` can be imported before `python_accounting.models`. It previously raised a circular `ImportError`.
- MySQL and MariaDB keep the microseconds of dates. MySQL previously rounded them to the second, so `Transaction.is_secure` failed for any Transaction dated half a second or more past the second.

### Documentation

- The quickstart and examples now run as written, and new pages cover install requirements, configuration, bulk assignment and repair (#38).

### Upgrading

- Entities whose year does not start in January: `python_accounting.utils.reporting_period_repair` finds the Reporting Periods opened early and the Balances attached to the wrong period by earlier versions. `find(session)` only lists them; `repair(session, findings)` applies the changes. Review the findings first: Balances are matched on `created_at`, which holds the time the application started rather than the time they were saved.
- MySQL and MariaDB: `create_all` does not alter existing tables, so change their DATETIME columns to DATETIME(6). Rows already rounded stay rounded, so Transactions affected by the bug will keep failing `is_secure`.

## 2.0.0 - 2026-10-16

### Breaking changes

- Python 3.11 or later is required. 1.1.0 is the last release supporting Python 3.9 and 3.10 (#30).
- SQLAlchemy 2.1 is required (>=2.1.4,<2.2) (#30).
- The strenum and toml dependencies are replaced by enum.StrEnum and tomllib from the standard library. Enum members behave as before, but a custom config file must now be valid TOML 1.0 (#33).

### Fixes

- Config.configure_dates sets the report date formats. It previously wrote them to the hashing configuration and had no effect on reports (#32).

### Maintenance

- Every dependency is upgraded to its latest release, and the package metadata moves to the [project] table (#30).

## 1.1.0 - 2026-10-16

The last minor release supporting Python 3.9 and 3.10.

- Python 3.13 and 3.14 support. The Python requirement is now >=3.9 rather than a list of versions.
- SQLAlchemy 2.1 support (now >=2.0.23,<2.2). Account codes, transaction numbers and reporting period counts were wrong on SQLAlchemy 2.1.
- Fix bulk assignment raising NegativeValueError when the assignable transaction runs out before the last outstanding transaction (#22).
- Account schedules and aging schedules only count assignments made up to the report end date (#21).
- Fix inclusive tax in transaction summaries (#26) and inclusive tax posting with integer rates (#27).
- Fix the indentation of the return statement in FinancialStatement._print_section (#17).
- Fix the config import in the README (#6).
- Releases are tested on Python 3.9 to 3.14 and published to PyPI from GitHub releases (#24).

